## Abstract

Edge fabrics must recover quickly from node failures, yet common orchestrators do not report how many simultaneous failures the current state is guaranteed to survive, and their recovery stalls when the control plane is partitioned away. We present CertiHeal-Edge, a self-healing framework in which an iterative neighbor-to-neighbor resistive field guides role reassignment as a capacitated flow toward spare capacity. It emits a repairability certificate: an exact per-scenario capacitated min-cut $C(F,S)$ and a design-time robust certificate of the tolerated simultaneous non-spare failures. At runtime the field produces a lower bound, valid under a two-phase reservation whose capacity-safety and no-double-booking properties we prove; issued as a capacity-checked reservation it is fail-closed. On a container fabric under a real control-plane partition, decentralized local-field recovery holds full role coverage across the certificate's feasible region while a central controller cannot reach the isolated site; a strong peer heuristic matches its coverage but double-books roles, which the reservation prevents. On a 16-node K3s testbed, reading the model certificate against live telemetry is unreliable and no capacity sum is fail-closed against a real bin-packing scheduler; a lease-based fence bounds the concurrent-execution window. Guarantees follow from the flow and min-cut structure, not from learning.

*Index Terms* — Self-healing, edge computing, service orchestration, repairability certificate, distributed min-cut, network and service management, fault tolerance, control-plane partition.

## I. Introduction

IoT and edge-fog fabrics run microservices, MEC services, and serverless functions on heterogeneous nodes that are often constrained by energy, temperature, and connectivity. Hardware failure and device degradation are routine at scale [47], and chaos engineering is now used to inject faults deliberately and expose weaknesses before a real incident occurs [59, 60]. The pressing operational question is then not only whether a system self-heals, but how many simultaneous failures its current spare capacity is guaranteed to repair, known before an incident rather than measured after the system has broken. From the viewpoint of network and service management this is an operational assurance about the fault tolerance of an edge region: every move of a role to another node is a management action, and a repairability certificate turns best-effort self-healing into a mechanism that can be checked and committed to.

A second question is where recovery is performed. When the control plane is reachable and spare capacity remains, the scheduler of a system such as Kubernetes reschedules failed pods within tens of seconds, so a second controller placed above it adds no measurable benefit. The difference appears only when an edge region loses contact with the control plane while the nodes inside it remain alive and can still reach one another. A recovery mechanism that depends on a central control plane cannot serve that region, whereas one that acts through local, peer-to-peer decisions can.

Several existing directions touch these questions but do not answer them fully.

- Kubernetes and its edge variants reschedule using free capacity, but they publish no certified tolerance level and still depend on a central or semi-central control node.
- Fault-injection studies [1, 58, 62] show that resilience depends strongly on the specific scenario, but this is a post-incident assessment.
- Orchestrators based on graph neural networks or multi-agent reinforcement learning [27, 29-32, 61] and on LLM agents [40] optimize expected cost; they do not certify which failure combinations remain recoverable and may lack a safe fallback on out-of-distribution inputs.

Research on verification and control offers stronger guarantees, but usually computes them offline or centrally and separates them from the recovery process. Self-stabilization [7], for example, guarantees that a system eventually returns to a legal state, but says nothing about how many simultaneous failures it can absorb.

CertiHeal-Edge connects these directions through two main contributions.

1. A repairability certificate with separated semantics. The exact per-scenario certificate $C(F,S)$ is the capacitated maximum flow (a capacitated min-cut) for the current failure set, proved tight in Appendix A; the a-priori certificate $C_{rob}(G,S)$, computed at design time, reports the number of simultaneous non-spare node failures that every combination of that size can still repair. Inside the field rounds the system computes a conservative lower bound $f_{out}(F,S) \le C(F,S)$, valid under a two-phase reservation whose capacity-safety and no-double-booking properties we prove; the fabric experiment issues it as a capacity-checked reservation, the fail-closed form in which the lower bound holds. $C(F,S)$ is nondecreasing when redundancy is added (in the nested sense), and the certificate comes with a training-free fallback (Sections IV-A and IV-C).

2. A distributed resistive-field recovery mechanism, coordinated by a per-region leader and a two-phase reservation, that reaches near-optimal assignment quality with low communication and, on a real partition, restores service where a central scheduler cannot (Sections IV-B, IV-C, and IV-E).

Two further mechanisms are analyzed in the Supplementary Material and kept outside the core guarantee: remaining-useful-life migration, which moves a role off a node before it fails and turns a correlated failure cluster into a sequence of individually easier repairs, evaluated as an oracle sensitivity study over prediction quality; and an optional local learning head that adjusts the field conductances, which yields only marginal improvement, while a reinforcement-learning variant performs worse than the plain field. That negative result supports letting the guarantees come from the structure of the problem rather than from learning.

The theoretical basis, including the simulated resistive field, vertex-disjoint routing, and decentralized cellular self-healing, is inherited from the author's doctoral dissertation [64]. The present paper carries that foundation to the IoT/edge orchestration layer and adds the repairability certificate and a decentralized recovery mechanism validated on real infrastructure.

## II. Related Work

The work relevant to CertiHeal-Edge spans communities that rarely meet: self-healing at the edge orchestration layer, formal verification and self-stabilizing control, distributed algorithms for the Laplacian and for min-cut, graph learning, safety-constrained learning, and predictive maintenance.

Fault tolerance at the edge orchestration layer has been approached from several loosely connected angles. Chaos engineering measures resilience after a fault has occurred [1]. Redundant service placement is typically modeled as a mixed-integer nonlinear program and solved centrally [2]. Markov chains and Petri nets give the long-run availability of MEC systems under failure and repair [33, 34, 36], and stateful service migration has been optimized, again centrally, to reduce interruption when nodes or users move [35, 37]; these models describe average probabilistic behavior, not a bound at a specific state on the number of simultaneous failures that can be repaired. Proactive schemes migrate early on anomaly detection, resource forecasting, or device failure prediction [3, 4, 26, 28, 43-46], and decentralized orchestration automates the service lifecycle across the cloud-edge continuum [5]. What remains open is an a-priori bound, computed from local data, on the number of simultaneous failures a region can still repair without returning to a centralized solver.

In self-adaptive systems, self-healing is usually organized as a monitor-analyze-plan-execute loop over a shared knowledge base, and recent work strengthens both ends of that loop: on the assurance side, runtime verification of changing requirements [38] and conformance checking of a MAPE-K design against its implementation [39]; on the execution side, LLM-driven microservice self-management [40], self-stabilizing orchestration across the edge-cloud continuum [42], and self-adaptive security [41]. All of them describe how to react to an incident; none issues a quantitative certificate of the fault margin that remains.

Verification and control provide tighter guarantees, but usually place the guarantee outside the recovery loop. Self-stabilizing designs guarantee convergence after transient faults within a bounded number of rounds, both for SDN control planes that communicate in-band [6] and for fog and edge systems [7]. Modular verification proves reachability through time-indexed invariants [8]. On the quantification of failures, the problem of finding the smallest set of links whose removal breaks reachability also reduces to a min-cut; one variant has been shown NP-hard and approximated by deep reinforcement learning [9]. Control barrier functions give physical guarantees at the level of a single cyber-physical loop [10]. CertiHeal-Edge differs in where the bound lives: a conservative flow-based lower bound is extracted inside the local field rounds, read as a lower bound on the number of repairable failures, proved nondecreasing under added redundancy, and coupled to a fallback. In [9] the computation is centralized, learned, and has no exact branch; self-stabilizing systems guarantee convergence but do not state how many simultaneous failures can be tolerated. No prior work combines a convergence guarantee with a min-cut capacity guarantee in one framework.

The distributed algorithmic components that CertiHeal-Edge uses rest on established results. A distributed Laplacian solver can compute potentials, currents, and effective resistance with near-optimal complexity [16]. Exact min-cut can be computed in $\tilde{O}(D+\sqrt{n})$ rounds, and in $\tilde{O}(D)$ on planar graphs [17], while small vertex cuts have been studied separately [18]. Dynamic algorithms maintain an approximate min-cut with sub-polynomial update cost [19, 20], and the toolbox also holds min-cost flow in the CONGEST model [49], sparsest cut via eigenvalue estimation [50], sketch-based dynamic connectivity [51], and surveys of distributed graph algorithms at scale [52]. The equivalent-circuit view of distributed optimization [48] links the Laplacian solution directly to the allocation mechanism the field exploits. All of these treat the cut value or the current as the final output; none uses the electrical field to steer recovery and allocate spare resources at the orchestration layer.

Graph learning borrows the same physics in the opposite direction. One line treats diffusion as an inductive bias: a GNN is a discretization of the diffusion equation [11], and learned Laplacian blocks can sit inside numerical integrators [12]. A second line uses the GNN as a solver for combinatorial optimization, relaxing the problem into a differentiable Hamiltonian [13], imitating SDP-based approximation algorithms or producing dual bounds [14, 15], reasoning in a primal-dual template [53], solving convex programs with guarantees [54], unrolling constrained optimization [55], or sampling solutions with diffusion models [56, 57]. At the orchestration layer, GNN and multi-agent reinforcement-learning schedulers place tasks, service chains, migrations, and autoscaling decisions to minimize expected cost [27, 29-32, 61]. In all of these the physics improves a learned solver or yields a global bound for a static, centralized problem; none produces a local flow lower bound, coupled to a fallback, that increases monotonically with redundancy on a running infrastructure. In our study the optional learning component yields only a marginal improvement (Supplementary Material).

Learning inside a safe region has been studied widely [21-25], mostly at the level of a single node: learn-to-optimize methods insert a differentiable feasibility step [21, 22], safe reinforcement learning shields the policy to preserve convergence or asymptotic optimality [23, 24], and deep equilibrium models come with a certified robustness radius [25]. These methods assume a given predictor, a global constraint set, or a central shield; decomposing the safe region into thousands of local regions tied to a topological certificate remains unaddressed.

## III. Method

### A. System Model and Problem Statement

The system is modeled as a service graph $G=(V,E)$ in which every vertex is an edge node and every role is a microservice or function that must run. When a node holding a role stops for good, that role becomes orphaned and must be moved to a spare that still has room. We state the recovery of a failure cluster as a capacitated flow problem. Because a fail-stopped node cannot participate in any computation, the failed set $F$ is removed, leaving the live graph $G_F=G[V\setminus F]$ on which the whole episode runs. Demand attaches through a two-level source that keeps each failed node's demand separate. A global super-source $\sigma_F$ connects to one per-node source $\sigma_v$ for each $v\in F$ by an edge of capacity $d_v$, the number of roles orphaned at $v$; each $\sigma_v$ has outgoing edges only, to the live neighbors $N(v)\setminus F$ of that node. No flow past $\sigma_v$ then exceeds $d_v$, a failed node's demand can leave only through its own surviving neighbors, and $\sigma_v$ cannot relay another node's demand or move demand across a disconnected component; the total demand is $d(F)=\sum_{v\in F} d_v$. Roles are interchangeable, any orphaned role may take any spare slot, so only the counts $d_v$ matter. A spare is a sink whose capacity is the number of additional roles it can accept, and every intermediate node has a finite capacity. A dead link has conductance zero. Once the surviving neighbors detect the failure by heartbeat, they initiate and run the episode; the failed nodes take no part.

The capacity of an intermediate node models two real orchestration constraints: the number of roles the node can host, which on Kubernetes is the allowed pod count per node and the main binding constraint during reassignment; and, when a role is stateful, the bandwidth or number of sessions the node relays while state is transferred along the migration path. Two recovery processes may therefore share a node up to its capacity. Stateless services are the special case in which no state is transferred, so recovery reduces to a direct assignment of roles to spares, a capacitated bipartite matching in which intermediate nodes are not consumed. Unit vertex-disjoint routing, one role per node and vertex-disjoint paths, is the other special case, in which every capacity equals one. For a stateful role the state must reach the recovery target from replication or external, replicated storage, since a fail-stopped node cannot serve its own state; the certificate then guarantees placement and relay capacity, not state recovery, and a role whose state is neither replicated nor externally stored can be re-placed but not restored. The fencing layer of Section IV-D addresses the dual hazard, two live replicas writing at once during a partition, and applies to stateless or idempotent load unless a token fence gates the replacement (Section V).

Splitting each node $v$ into an in-out pair $(v_{in}, v_{out})$ joined by an edge of capacity $b_v$, the maximum number of roles recovered at once equals the maximum integral flow from $\sigma_F$ to the sink set $S$ in $G_F$, and by the max-flow min-cut theorem (the capacitated form of Menger's theorem) equals the minimum cut separating $\sigma_F$ from $S$ in that network (Theorem 1); this is the quantity $C(F,S)$ of Section III-C. The model captures one capacity dimension, the node-hosting capacity $b_v$ measured in role slots; when a role is stateful, the relay bandwidth along a migration path is expressed in the same unit by normalizing one slot to the bandwidth a single relayed role consumes, so node hosting and link relay share a common capacity unit. Multi-resource constraints such as multi-dimensional CPU and RAM, affinity and anti-affinity, taints, fixed stateful volumes, and service dependency chains are outside the model (Section V).

At a given instant, $S$ is the set of spares and $F$ the set of failed nodes, with $F$ removed from the live subgraph. Each live edge $(v,w)$ of $G_F$ has a conductance $c_{vw}$ in a positive range. The potential field $u$ satisfies a weighted discrete Laplace equation on $G_F$ with Dirichlet boundary $u=1$ on the super-source $\sigma_F$ and $u=0$ on the spares $S$; the per-node sources $\sigma_v$ are internal and inject their current into the neighbors $N(v)\setminus F$. Every live node other than a spare is interior, so its potential equals the weighted average of its neighbors' potentials, the injected nodes $N(v)\setminus F$ included. The nominal conductance $c^0$ is fixed and independent of the learning component; it defines the training-free fallback branch.

The central evaluation quantity is $\kappa(t)$, the probability that the system reassigns all roles when $t$ nodes fail at once and the full spare capacity may be used. The failure model is fail-stop: a failed node halts and does not keep running incorrectly. Failures are detected by a local heartbeat such as a lease or a probe, in an asynchronous setting where nodes join and leave. The evaluation metrics are $\kappa(t)$, the fraction of services recovered, latency, the number of rounds to convergence, and the amount of data exchanged per node.

### B. Design

On each node, CertiHeal-Edge iterates five steps: sense, control, solve the field, check, and fall back. A node gathers telemetry from its one-hop neighbors, including energy level, load, RSSI, and a locally computed RUL or anomaly score; sets the conductance of its edges and identifies the source and sink boundary; performs one relaxation step of the Laplace equation, exchanging only with direct neighbors, to update the potential and the current; and uses the current field to select spares and trace the direction of largest current, reserving spare capacity along the path to form a feasible assignment that respects node capacity. An optional learned head may adjust the conductance at the control step; it takes no part in the core guarantee.

The check step is where recovery meets the certificate. A node builds the assignment with both the learned and the nominal conductance $c^0$ in shadow mode, then keeps the assignment that serves more demand, $f_{out}=\max(f_\theta, f_0)$; this value is a recovery witness, a lower bound on $C(F,S)$ valid under the reservation invariants stated below. If $f_{out}$ meets the required threshold and the path latency stays within its bound, the node commits that assignment. Because the nominal branch does not depend on the learned model, $f_{out}(F,S) \ge f_0(F,S)$ on every scenario (Theorem 2). Algorithm 1 describes the per-node loop.

```
Algorithm 1  HEAL_EPISODE: one episode, run by the SURVIVING nodes after a failure is detected
Input: failed set F (removed -> live graph G_F=G[V\F]), spares S, orphaned roles R(F) in role-id order,
       role demand q_r, episode id e (assigned at detection; carried on every message for idempotence)
Two modes: CERTIFIED uses nominal conductance c0, a hard Dirichlet boundary, and lambda=0 (Thm 2, Lem 1).
           HEURISTIC uses learned conductance g_theta, soft sources, and lambda>0 (best effort, no bound).
// PHASE A - field on G_F (each surviving node v, one-hop exchange with N(v), to convergence):
1  rho_v <- RUL_predict(history_v)                                    // heuristic mode only
2  exchange u_v with N(v)
3  boundary: u=1 on super-source sigma_F; per-node source sigma_v (cap d_v) injects into N(v)\F; u=0 on spares S
4  CERTIFIED: hard boundary (step 3), s_v=0 at interior nodes, lambda=0, c_vw=c0_vw
