Algorithm 1 is a single episode: $f_\theta$, $f_0$, and $f_{out}$ are properties of the whole scenario, so the two branches route on two separate virtual copies of the spare-capacity state, and Phase C uses a distributed aggregation, a spanning tree or gossip, to sum the flow of each branch, select the winner, then atomically commit that branch's reservations and release the rest. The cost of this aggregation is included in the round and byte counts of Section IV-A.

The witness bound $f_{field}\le C(F,S)$ rests on a two-phase reservation over the resource units a role's recovery consumes. Recovering role $r$ reserves a whole path: a spare slot, and the in-out capacity of each intermediate node and each link the migration crosses. Every such unit has a state FREE, PREPARED, or COMMITTED, keyed by $(e,\text{role},\text{resource})$ with $e$ the episode id, so a retried or delayed message is idempotent. In the prepare phase the coordinator walks $r$'s path and sends $\text{RESERVE}(e,r,\text{resource})$ to each unit; a unit moves FREE $\to$ PREPARED only if its free capacity admits $r$'s demand, and a RESERVE that finds the unit already held for a different role is refused, the fail-closed response. Commit is path-atomic: only after every unit on $r$'s path acknowledges PREPARED does the coordinator send COMMIT to all of them, moving each PREPARED $\to$ COMMITTED, and the role is recovered, its workload activated, only at this COMMIT-ALL point. A duplicate or delayed COMMIT is a no-op under the same key. If any unit cannot be prepared, or a lease expires before COMMIT-ALL, the whole path aborts and every PREPARED and COMMITTED unit of that role reverts to FREE, so no committed prefix is stranded. A role counts toward $f_{field}$ only when its path is COMMIT-ALL; a partially committed path contributes nothing.

Coordinator failure advances the epoch. The coordinator is itself a node, so its death orphans its own roles and enlarges the failure set; the surviving lowest-index node therefore does not re-run episode $e$ but opens episode $e+1$, inheriting the COMMITTED records of $e$, recomputing $F$, $d(F)$, and the certificate for the new failure set, keeping every role already COMMIT-ALL, and garbage-collecting PREPARED units (which time out to FREE) and the committed prefix of any unfinished path. Each unit's $(e,\text{role},\text{resource})$ record is what the new coordinator reads to tell a finished path from a prefix.

Three safety properties follow by induction over messages. Capacity safety: a unit admits a prepare only while free capacity remains, and releases a committed unit only on a whole-path abort, so its committed load never exceeds $b_v$. No double-booking: a unit leaves FREE for exactly one $(e,\text{role})$ and only that owner commits it, so no unit serves two roles. Path atomicity: a role's units are all COMMITTED or all FREE, since commit is issued only after every unit is PREPARED and any failure aborts the whole path. Under the three, the COMMIT-ALL routes form a feasible flow in $G_F$, so $f_{field}\le C(F,S)$ by weak duality. Progress is weaker: leases return every PREPARED unit to FREE or COMMITTED so nothing is stuck, but two coordinators contending after a partition heals can still livelock a role across epochs, which the single lowest-index coordinator per region removes on the tested topology. The coordinator-kill runs of Section IV-C exercise this path on the container fabric; a machine-checked proof over the full message interleaving, and a retry bound under contention, are future work.

Three layers coexist: the learned branch and the training-free nominal branch, both field heuristics that guarantee only $f_{out} \ge f_0$, and the exact max-flow solver used when neither covers the demand. To reach a global optimum the exact solver may reroute the field's PREPARED but not-yet-activated reservations, augmenting through them, so it runs before COMMIT-ALL; in the prototype it runs centrally, which gives up the low-communication property on the hardest scenarios. Section IV-E reports the plain field without this solver, against a separate augmenting-path solver used as an optimality ceiling.

Two modes realize the model of Section III-A. The guaranteed branch (CERTIFIED mode in Algorithm 1) fixes the Dirichlet boundary at $u=1$ on the virtual super-source $\sigma_F$ and $u=0$ on $S$ in $G_F$ with $\lambda=0$; it solves exactly the Laplace problem of Section III-A, so every statement in Appendix A applies to the running configuration. The normal operating mode uses soft sources and sinks, $s_v$ positive on the recovery boundary $B_F$, where $\sigma_F$ injects the demand, and negative at spares, which suits step-by-step Jacobi updates in an asynchronous environment. It solves a Poisson system $L_c\,u=s$, and as the source magnitude $M$ on $B_F$ and $S$ grows the soft solution approaches the Dirichlet solution with a potential difference bounded as $O(1/M)$ by a standard penalty argument. A small potential difference does not by itself fix the extracted route when two edges carry nearly equal current: if at every interior node the largest current exceeds the second largest by a fixed margin $\delta>0$, then for large enough $M$ the current order, and therefore the reservation route, matches the Dirichlet solution; without that margin only the potential converges, and the guaranteed branch uses the hard boundary and does not depend on this argument. The term $\lambda\rho_v$ is a failure-prediction potential for proactive migration: it steers current away from nodes with low RUL before they fail. It is a bounded source perturbation proportional to $\lambda$ and carries no min-cut guarantee; every statement about the certificate and the fallback is taken at $\lambda=0$, and $\lambda>0$ is tested only empirically (Supplementary Material). The thresholds $C_{req}$, $L_{max}$, and $\lambda$ are operator policy parameters, and the system is not knife-edge sensitive to them: a higher $C_{req}$ only makes a node more conservative and fall back more often, and the training-free fallback bounds the worst case at $f_0(F,S)$.

### C. Repairability Certificate and Analysis

The certificate uses a classical result in a new operational way. Four quantities are kept distinct.

- $C(F,S)$, the exact per-scenario certificate. For a specific failure set $F$ and spare set $S$, this is the value of the capacitated maximum flow from the virtual-source construction to $S$ on $G_F$, equal to the capacitated $\sigma_F$-$S$ min-cut (Theorem 1; vertex-disjoint routing is the unit-capacity case). It is the maximum number of orphaned roles that can be reassigned at once in that scenario, and it does not decrease when spares are added.
- $f_{field}(F,S)$, a recovery witness: the number of roles the field actually assigns. This lower bound is conditional on the three reservation invariants of Section III-B; under them the recovery routes form a feasible flow and by weak duality $f_{field}(F,S) \le C(F,S)$. The system emits this witness from the local field rounds. Computing $C(F,S)$ exactly in a distributed way is expensive: the best distributed exact global min-cut runs in about $\tilde{O}(D+\sqrt{n})$ rounds [17], and the recovery certificate is a capacitated multi-source multi-sink s-t min-cut, a generally costlier problem for which no comparably cheap distributed algorithm is known. Because the procedure is greedy, $f_{field}$ can be smaller than $C(F,S)$ (self-stranding, measured in Section IV-E): it exhibits a feasible recovery of a given size without certifying the maximum.
- $C_{rob}(G,S)$, the a-priori robust certificate. This is the quantity that holds before it is known which nodes will fail. Because a node $v$ can carry $d_v$ roles, the total demand from a failure set $F$ is $d(F)=\sum_{v\in F} d_v$. We measure the failure budget by the number of failed nodes $t$ and define $C_{rob}(G,S)=\max\{t : \forall F\subseteq V\setminus S,\ |F|\le t,\ C(F,S)\ge d(F)\}$, the largest number of simultaneous non-spare failures for which every combination of that size still fully recovers all demand; in the unit case $d_v=1$ the condition reduces to $C(F,S)\ge |F|$. The definition restricts failures to the non-spare nodes; if spares can also fail, the sink set becomes $S\setminus F$ and the robust certificate is correspondingly smaller, a stricter variant that the values reported here do not use. Because it is a minimum over the whole class of failure sets, it is a worst-case connectivity property.
- $\kappa(t)$, the empirical success probability, a real number in $[0,1]$: the fraction of $t$-failure combinations that recover, averaged over the failure distribution. This is average performance, not a certificate.

$C(F,S)$ and the witness $f_{field}(F,S)$ are per-scenario: they concern the failures already observed and are available before the recovery commits, so a node knows it can repair before it reassigns. Only $C_{rob}(G,S)$ is an a-priori commitment to tolerate any $t$ failures; because it takes a minimum over all $F$, it is computed offline at design time as a property of the infrastructure. The two are complementary: $C_{rob}$ gives a design margin published before the incident, and $f_{field}$ a cheap local confirmation at each incident. If $C_{rob}(G,S)\ge t$, then for every $F$ with $|F|\le t$ we have $C(F,S)\ge d(F)$, so full recovery is guaranteed.

All four quantities are defined the same way in the capacitated network (Section III-A): when each node carries at most $b$ roles, the in-out edge has capacity $b$, and the maximum flow replaces unit vertex-disjoint flow. The predictive accuracy of the certificate in real operation, the rate at which it correctly predicts whether roles will hang, is measured separately in Section IV-D.

The fallback branch has its own guarantee. With the nominal conductance $c^0$, the current-tracing and capacity-reservation procedure always produces a valid assignment that serves at least $f_0(F,S)$ units of demand for the scenario at hand; because the gate takes $f_{out}=\max(f_\theta, f_0)$, the result cannot be worse than the non-learned branch (Theorem 2). The field is a fixed point of the Jacobi iteration and converges in $O(\log(1/\varepsilon)/(1-\rho))$ rounds, where $\rho<1$ is the spectral radius of the Dirichlet Jacobi matrix (Lemma 1). With an asynchronous schedule, an update gap bounded by $B$, and message delay bounded by $\Delta$, the time to reach an $\varepsilon$-accurate fixed point is $O((B+\Delta)\log(1/\varepsilon)/(1-\rho))$ (Lemma 2). The analysis assumes a mesh or near-planar graph, connected per component, fail-stop faults, and conductances bounded in $[c_{min},c_{max}]$ with $c_{min}>0$ on the live subgraph. Appendix A gives proof sketches.

### D. Evaluation Methodology

The evaluation uses simulation and a testbed. The simulation runs on a CPU and measures $\kappa(t)$ on grids and random geometric graphs, the relation between the certificate and redundancy, the Jacobi convergence rate, and scalability with size; the effect of proactive migration and an ablation over the GNN step count $K$ are reported in the Supplementary Material. The comparison methods are an uncoordinated discrete algorithm, a centralized greedy solver, and an optimal solution. Table 1 lists the main hyperparameters of the simulation and the testbed.

