| Min-cut certificate | grid size / number of spares | 6x6 / 4 to 16 |
| Fabric partition (Sec. IV-C) | nodes (co-located + edge) / roles / capacity $b$ / seeds | 15 (3 + 12) / 42 / 4 / 5 |
| K3s prototype (Sec. IV-D) | nodes on two WAN machines / roles / per-node cap; detection | 16 / 64 / 6; grace-period $\approx$ 40 s |
| K3s admission sweep (Sec. IV-D) | pods (small 3 CPU + big 9 CPU) / node CPU / cordon levels $L$ / episodes | 45 (30 + 15), 225 CPU / 32 / $L$ = 6 to 12 / 18 |

Validation uses two platforms. The first is a decentralized container fabric built to isolate the loss-of-control-plane regime: each node is an agent holding a set of roles under a capacity limit $b$; two virtual networks separate the control channel (agent to control plane) from the peer channel (agent to agent); a real partition disconnects an edge region from the control channel while the region keeps its peer channel; and recovery is run by a local coordinator elected within the region over the peer channel, with no control plane. Roles are local to their region, modeling edge data locality, so a partitioned control plane cannot move a role elsewhere (Section IV-C). The second is a k3d/K3s cluster of 16 nodes across two physical machines connected over the internet by a mesh VPN, with roles packaged as pods carrying resource requests so that spare capacity is meaningful; it measures what the fabric cannot: certificate accuracy under real scheduling noise, robustness over a three-hour random chaos schedule of 180 node kills, and split-brain behavior under a real wide-area partition (Section IV-D). On the same cluster an admission sweep tests the certificate as a predictor under a bin-packing scheduler: the workload is heterogeneous, thirty pods at three CPU and fifteen at nine (225 CPU on sixteen 32-CPU nodes), so the uniform-slot certificate can over-count; we cordon $L$ nodes for $L$ from six to twelve, re-place the whole workload with a rollout restart, let the pending count settle, and count an episode as a hang if any pod stays pending. Three decision rules, the slot certificate, a real-CPU sum, and observation of the actual placement, are scored on the same eighteen episodes. Failures are injected by stopping a node's container (node loss); cutting a connection (real partition); and blocking traffic to the control plane, with iptables on K3s and network disconnection on the fabric, which keeps the node alive but out of contact (split-brain). On K3s a lease-based fencing layer freezes a node that has lost contact beyond a threshold before its replacement takes over. At the simulation layer an augmenting-path solver (Edmonds-Karp) on the node-split graph serves as an optimal adversary for the gap to optimum, the self-stranding rate, and the certificate under the capacitated model.

The three implementations differ in how decentralized they are. The simulation implements Algorithm 1 exactly, each node keeping only local state, updating the field by one-hop exchange, and building routes from neighborhood information; it is where rounds to convergence, bytes exchanged, quality relative to optimum, and self-stranding are measured. The container fabric runs the same peer recovery on a real container system, with an elected in-region coordinator and no control plane intervening. The K3s prototype does not run the peer loop: a centralized controller reads cluster state through the Kubernetes API and calls kubectl, and failure detection relies on the node-monitor-grace-period of Kubernetes; a decentralized neighbor lease is the next step.

## IV. Results

### A. Certificate and Convergence Rate

The min-cut results match the analysis. On a 6 by 6 grid, as the number of spares grows from 4 to 16, the mean min-cut rises from 3.92 to 4.49 and $\kappa$ rises with it (Fig. 1). The communication cost of the field grows slowly with size. On a grid of diameter 14, Jacobi needs about 34 rounds, roughly 2.4 times the diameter. On random geometric graphs the ratio of rounds to diameter falls from 3.7 to 1.96 as $n$ grows. The $O(D^2\log(1/\varepsilon))$ worst case of Lemma 1 is loose for grids: the measured round count is nearly linear in the diameter. For data volume, the potential payload of the field for one recovery is about 0.9 KB per node (34 rounds times about 3.3 neighbors times 8 bytes), excluding telemetry, reservation and commit messages, and headers. The per-node potential traffic is constant in size, $O(\deg)$, while a centralized decision reads the whole cluster state, about 98 KB on a small cluster and 398 KB on the 16-node fabric, close to linear in size. Each Jacobi round at a node is a weighted average over neighbors, $O(\deg)$ multiply-add operations, a few hundred operations per node for one recovery, under a millisecond on an embedded CPU, with $O(\deg)$ state.

The a-priori certificate $C_{rob}$ is computed exactly by brute force on the 6 by 6 grid: for each placement of spares we find the smallest failure set that removes full recovery of the demand, and $C_{rob}$ is that size minus one. For a nested sequence of placements $S_4 \subseteq S_6 \subseteq \dots \subseteq S_{16}$ (adding spares in farthest-point order), $C_{rob}$ is nondecreasing, at 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 4 as $m$ grows from 4 to 16, as Theorem 1 predicts, since adding a sink cannot lower the max-flow on any failure combination. When each $m$ instead uses a separately redesigned, non-nested spread, $C_{rob}$ can fall as $m$ grows (2, 2, 3, 3, 2, 4): monotonicity holds for nested placements, and a redesigned placement can leave a vulnerable pocket in the worst case. In both sequences $C_{rob}$ is below the mean $C(F,S)$ (4 against 4.95 at $m=16$); only $C_{rob}$ is a commitment for every failure combination. The brute force is combinatorial, needing $O(\sum_{i \le C_{rob}+1} \binom{n}{i})$ max-flow computations, feasible for a fabric of tens of nodes and confirmed at $n=16$; at production scale, cut enumeration, branch-and-bound, an integer program, or a fast conservative bound such as local connectivity is needed.

