### B. Decentralized Recovery

On grid graphs, the uncoordinated discrete algorithm drops sharply when failures arrive in clusters, whereas the field tracks both the centralized greedy method and the optimal solution (Fig. 2). The gap to optimum, measured directly against the augmenting-path solver in Section IV-E, is small at low $t$ and widens as $t$ grows. On non-uniform random geometric graphs the field even exceeds the greedy method: at $n=300$, $\kappa$ reaches 0.263 against 0.113, while each round uses only one-hop exchange. The greedy method runs a global search per source; the field reaches comparable quality through one-hop exchange in a bounded number of rounds, so its advantage is coordination cost.

