### C. Control-Plane Partition: Decentralized Against Centralized Recovery

When the central control plane itself disappears, the only regime where a mechanism above the Kubernetes scheduler can add availability (Section I), what matters is whether the region recovers on its own. To isolate that regime we built a container fabric (Section III-D) in which the partition, node death, and reassignment are all real operations.

The fabric has 15 agents, each holding a set of roles under capacity $b=4$. Three agents are co-located with the control plane (region $s_0$, never partitioned, a control) and twelve form an edge region $s_1$ carrying 36 roles at about 75% of capacity. Roles are local: a role of $s_1$ can only run on an agent in $s_1$, which models edge data locality, so when $s_1$ loses the control plane the control plane cannot move a role to another region. Two virtual networks separate the control channel from the peer channel. A real partition is created by disconnecting all of $s_1$ from the control channel while the agents keep the peer channel, after which $k$ edge nodes are killed. Three recovery modes are compared on exactly the same failure schedule. The baseline relies only on the central control plane. The peer heuristic is a strong decentralized comparator: every surviving agent in $s_1$ independently reassigns orphaned roles over the peer channel to the least-loaded reachable peer, respecting the capacity $b$, but with no elected coordinator, no reservation, and no certificate. CertiHeal adds coordination and the certificate to that same local recovery: the surviving agents elect a local coordinator, the lowest-index survivor, which reassigns orphaned roles over the peer channel under a capacity-checked reservation and needs no control plane. The peer heuristic isolates what local recovery achieves on its own. In this degenerate star the certificate reduces to a capacity check: full recovery is feasible if and only if the surviving capacity of the region holds all roles, that is $(12-k)\,b \ge 36$, equivalently $k \le 3$. We sweep $k$ from 1 to 6, five seeds per level, and measure role coverage, the fraction of roles served by at least one live agent, together with the number of double-booked roles, those held by more than one live agent.

The three modes separate cleanly (Table 2, Fig. 3). With the control plane lost, the baseline cannot reassign the orphaned roles in the partitioned region because locality forbids moving them elsewhere: coverage falls linearly with the number of dead nodes, from 92.9% at $k=1$ to 57.1% at $k=6$, and does not recover during the partition. CertiHeal recovers locally over the peer channel and holds exactly 100% coverage across the whole region the certificate declares feasible ($k \le 3$), then tracks the remaining capacity beyond it, at 90.5%, 81.0%, and 71.4% for $k=4, 5, 6$. The peer heuristic recovers far more than the baseline, since it too acts locally, but does not match CertiHeal in the feasible region: its mean coverage is 100%, 98.6%, and 96.7% for $k=1, 2, 3$, and it varies across seeds, dropping to 35 of 42 roles in the worst feasible run.

Across all 30 CertiHeal runs (five seeds by six levels), the confusion matrix between "certificate says feasible" and "recovers fully" is $\text{TP}=15$, $\text{FP}=0$, $\text{TN}=15$, $\text{FN}=0$; the false-safe rate, the probability of a hung role when the certificate declares success, is zero over the 15 feasible configurations. The outcome is deterministic in $k$ on this topology, so the five seeds reproduce it exactly; with 15 feasible configurations the rule of three places the 95% upper limit on the false-safe rate at about 20%. When the real constraints match the capacitated flow model, the model certificate is an operational commitment. The recovery time of CertiHeal grows with the number of roles to reassign, from about 6 s at $k=1$ to about 27 s at $k=6$ (Fig. 3), the coordination time of a paced local-coordinator loop.

The peer heuristic shows why coordination is needed for the certificate to hold. Lacking a reservation, two surviving agents can adopt the same orphaned role: across the 30 peer runs 32 roles were double-booked, up to 14 at $k=1$, whereas CertiHeal double-booked none. Those duplicate placements consume capacity, so the peer heuristic strands other roles and fails to recover fully in 2 of its 15 model-certified configurations, a 13% failure rate within the region the model certificate calls feasible (the peer heuristic issues no certificate of its own, so this is not a false-safe rate), against 0 of 15 for CertiHeal. Local recovery on its own restores most of the coverage; the coordinated, capacity-checked reservation is what makes the certificate a commitment that holds and keeps each role on a single node. The baseline and CertiHeal coverage are deterministic across the five seeds, the baseline losing exactly $3k$ roles and CertiHeal recovering to the capacity ceiling of the region; the peer heuristic alone varies with the seed, because its outcome depends on the order in which the uncoordinated agents claim roles.

The reservation must also survive coordinator failure. With $k$ nodes failed already ($k=1$ or $2$) and recovery under way, we kill the acting coordinator, the lowest-index survivor, which orphans its own roles and brings the total to $k+1$ failures, still within the certificate. Over five seeds at each $k$, all ten runs recovered to full 42 of 42 coverage and double-booked no role, so the epoch advance and the per-unit $(e,\text{role},\text{resource})$ records recover a partial commit without losing or duplicating an assignment, the coordinator-failure case of Section III-B. Recovery time is the coordination time of the new coordinator, which also places the dead coordinator's roles: mean 5.8 s (range 4.1 to 6.3) at $k=1$ and 27.4 s (24.6 to 29.0) at $k=2$.

