### D. Validation on a Real Kubernetes Cluster: Operational Certificate, Split-Brain, and Fencing

The fabric of Section IV-C isolates the decentralized mechanism but runs on a separate harness. Two quantities appear only on a real orchestrator: the accuracy of the certificate under scheduling noise, and split-brain behavior under partition. We deploy the mechanism on a real k3d/K3s cluster (Section III-D), extended to a 16-node fabric across two physical machines connected over the internet by a mesh VPN (Tailscale, WAN latency 70 to 90 ms; three nodes on the first machine, thirteen on the second), running 64 roles.

On this platform we inject a chaos schedule of 180 random node kills over three hours, generated in sync on both machines from a shared seed: every 45 to 150 s one to three nodes are killed, occasionally up to seven at once, rejoining after 30 to 180 s. Over the three hours the system is at full 64 of 64 roles for 93.6% of the time, with a median reassignment time of about 2 s that is nearly independent of the number of simultaneous failures (detection time remains bounded by the node-monitor-grace-period of Kubernetes). Certificate accuracy is scored per failure episode, a contiguous window with at least one failed node, because samples within an episode are strongly autocorrelated; the run contained 52 episodes. Counting an episode as a hang when a role stays unplaceable for at least three consecutive samples, about the reassignment time, the episode-level confusion between the certificate verdict and the outcome is 42 true positives, 6 false-safe episodes, 0 true negatives, and 4 false negatives. Under the strictest definition, any pending role at all, the false-safe count rises to 17 of 48 within-certificate episodes. Roles become unplaceable when the failures exceed the certificate, up to 31 at the peak (Fig. 4).

$C(F,S)$ is exact in the modeled capacitated flow network, and in the fabric of Section IV-C, where every assignment is accepted by a real capacity check, its false-safe rate is zero. Read against a live orchestrator the same quantity is an unreliable predictor: the episode-level false-safe rate runs from 12 percent at the three-sample threshold (6 of 48 within-certificate episodes) to 35 percent under the strictest definition, and specificity is near zero because most episodes beyond the certificate still recover without a sustained hang. The episode set is imbalanced, 48 of 52 episodes lying within the certificate, so the four beyond-certificate episodes are too few to estimate specificity; a schedule that dwells beyond the certificate boundary, run several times with an episode-level confidence interval, is needed to characterize the infeasible class. Scheduling order and timing, which the flow model does not capture, drive this gap.

The admission sweep (Section III-D) separates a capacity check from an actual placement on the same cluster. The slot certificate over-promises: across eighteen episodes it declares feasible in thirteen and hangs in ten (TP 3, FP 10, TN 5, FN 0), a 77 percent false-safe rate. Summing the real CPU requests is stricter but still over-promises in half its feasible calls (TP 3, FP 3, TN 12, FN 0), because placement is bin-packing and no capacity sum sees fragmentation: nine-CPU pods that do not fit a partly filled node. The third rule, declaring feasible only after observing that nothing stays pending, has no false-safe outcome (TP 3, TN 15) by construction; it is a post-admission witness read after the pods are placed, not a certificate issued before commit. No capacity sum is therefore fail-closed on a real scheduler. A pre-placement guarantee on Kubernetes needs a reservation that holds capacity before activation, such as reservation pods or a scheduler reserve plugin that binds only after the whole batch is admitted and rolls back otherwise; the fabric reservation of Section IV-C is that mechanism on a separate harness, and building it on Kubernetes is future work.

A real wide-area partition also exposes a safety problem that stopping a container does not show. When the second machine is disconnected, its edge nodes stay alive and keep running their old roles while the control plane creates replacements, so during the partition window the number of concurrently running replicas doubles; for a stateful service, two writing replicas can corrupt data. We add a lease-based fencing layer: a node that has lost contact with the control plane beyond a lease threshold freezes itself and stops serving before its replacement takes over. Measured directly on the same partition, without fencing the isolated side serves throughout the partition (41 roles alongside 18 replacements), the concurrent-execution window lasts the full 161 s and is in principle unbounded in the length of the outage; with fencing the isolated side stops after the lease expires, and the window shrinks to about 49 s and then to zero for the rest of the partition (Fig. 5). The layer trades a pause bounded by the lease threshold for an upper bound on concurrent-execution exposure.

