### E. Strong Algorithmic Adversary: Gap to Optimum and the Limit of Local Recovery

We implement a vertex-disjoint flow solver by augmenting paths (Edmonds-Karp) on the node-split graph of the field; it reaches the maximum vertex-disjoint flow, equal to the min-cut, and is an optimal ceiling. On an 8 by 8 grid with clustered failures the field tracks this ceiling at small $t$ but falls away as $t$ grows (Fig. 6). The fraction of configurations where the field falls below the optimum, self-stranding through sequential reservation, is 0% at $t \le 2$, rising to 33.0% at $t=6$ and 52.0% at $t=8$. The augmenting-path solver recovers these configurations and reaches the optimum, but must find a global flow: the mean number of edges traversed rises from 55 to 565 with $t$, while the field uses about 32 to 37 rounds of one-hop exchange, nearly constant in $t$.

A second experiment tests the capacity model. The unit vertex-disjoint model assumes one role per node, while the testbed lets a node carry up to six pods. Splitting a node into an in-out pair with an inner edge of capacity $c$ makes the min-cut a certificate under the capacitated model. The certificate rises almost linearly with node capacity: means of 5.09, 10.28, 15.20, 20.50, and 30.30 for $c$ equal to 1, 2, 3, 4, and 6 (Fig. 6). At the testbed capacity of 6 the certificate is about 30, so the unit-capacity model underestimates the repairability of the fabric by about six times. Capacity does not simply scale the certificate: in a controlled bridge example (six sources through two relay nodes to six spares), the certificate equals $\min$(bridge, bind constraint) and tracks the tightest constraint, the bridge link capacity when the link is the bottleneck (2, 4, 6, 6 for link capacities 2, 4, 6, 8) and the relay node capacity when the node is.

The guarantee of the fallback branch (Theorem 2) is tested directly. Running the nominal branch in parallel with an imperfect learned branch (noisy conductance) and taking $f_{out}=\max(f_\theta, f_0)$, on 500 clustered-failure configurations $f_{out} \ge f_0$ holds 100% by construction, and in 14.6% of the configurations the nominal branch serves more demand than the learned branch, so the max is a real safeguard (the learned branch is better in only 2.8%). The mean gap from $f_{out}$ to the exact $C(F,S)$ is 0.1 role, at the cost of running the field solve twice per episode.

