## V. Discussion

Across the simulations, the local field tracks centralized orchestrators while exchanging only with one-hop neighbors, and the min-cut certificate provides a bound that neither reactive Kubernetes nor learned orchestrators emit. Proactive migration uses the meaning of the certificate: it turns a cluster of simultaneous failures into a sequence of events, which moves the system into an easier region of the $\kappa(t)$ curve. The learning component contributes only at the margin, and the reinforcement-learning variant is worse than the plain field.

Decentralized recovery pays off only when the central control plane disappears (Section I): under a partition the central scheduler cannot reach the edge region and the orphaned roles there stay down, while peer-to-peer recovery restores them from intra-region communication alone (Section IV-C). The peer heuristic shows that local recovery, not the certificate, is what beats a partitioned scheduler; the coordinated reservation adds the commitment, full recovery throughout the certified-feasible region with no double-booking, where the uncoordinated heuristic strands roles and places some on two nodes at once. Issued as a capacity-checked reservation, the certificate declared no false success on any fabric scenario; its lower accuracy against live Kubernetes telemetry (Section IV-D) reflects unmodeled scheduling order and bin-packing, not the theorem.

Several limitations bound these results.

The certificate rests on a classical min-cut; if the mechanism reduces to a min-cost flow with potentials, the contribution is the local computation, the operational reading, and the fallback, not a faster solver. The model carries one capacity dimension in role slots; multi-dimensional CPU and RAM, affinity, taints, fixed volumes, and dependency chains are outside it, and Section IV-D shows the cost of that gap under a bin-packing scheduler. The field does not beat an optimal solver or an integer program: it self-strands at a rate that grows with the number of failures, past half the configurations at the largest tested load, the price of one-hop exchange in place of a global flow (Section IV-E).

The failure model covers fail-stop only; Byzantine faults, where a node keeps running but returns wrong or dishonest results, and silent failures are out of scope. The testbed ran on a 16-node fabric across two physical machines over the internet, with a real network partition and three hours of random chaos (Sections IV-C, IV-D), but it is smaller than a production deployment of hundreds of nodes, and the test load is lightweight busybox pods, so the absolute numbers reflect orchestration latency, not application startup.

The container fabric is a separate harness rather than a production edge autonomy framework, and the K3s prototype runs a centralized controller with Kubernetes failure detection (Section III-D), so the fully decentralized loop has been validated on the fabric only.

Fencing bounds the concurrent-execution window at the lease threshold but does not remove the overlap, so the write-safety result holds for stateless or idempotent load; zero overlap for stateful services needs a token fence that gates activation of the replacement by the lease, or a remote power or network cutoff.

Three directions follow. The first is to place CertiHeal-Edge on a production edge autonomy framework such as KubeEdge or OpenYurt, with real workloads and traces, a token fence for stateful load, a decentralized neighbor lease for detection, and a pre-placement reservation on the Kubernetes scheduler; these frameworks provide disconnection autonomy but emit no certificate and perform no fencing, so the certificate and the fencing layer sit above them. The second is to evaluate the learning component on larger graphs or longer distances, to determine whether the room the oracle reveals becomes a real benefit. The third is the redundancy-placement problem: how many spares are needed to guarantee repair of $t$ failures on a class of graphs. A result of that kind would turn the certificate from an observation tool into a tool for infrastructure design.

## VI. Conclusion

CertiHeal-Edge is a self-healing framework in which a decentralized field algorithm recovers failures from local data alone, emits a repairability certificate with a fallback, and can migrate roles before a node fails. In simulation the field tracks centralized orchestrators with one-hop exchange; on a real container fabric, decentralized recovery restores service when the control plane is partitioned away from an edge region, with the capacity-checked reservation acting as a fail-closed check; and on real Kubernetes the model certificate read against live telemetry is not fail-closed, while the lease-based fence bounds concurrent execution under a wide-area partition. Because the guarantee is a property of capacitated flow, min-cut, and the electrical field rather than of a learned model, an operator can publish a design-time tolerance level $C_{rob}$ for simultaneous non-spare failures before any incident and check the per-scenario recovery witness at each one, which turns best-effort self-healing into a managed and checkable service. The next step is to carry the decentralized agent and a pre-placement reservation onto a production edge framework with real workloads and a token fence for stateful services.

## Appendix A. Proof Sketches

Theorem 1 (certificate: tight value, monotonicity, and cheap lower bound). Work on the live graph $G_F=G[V\setminus F]$, and split each node $v$ into an in-node $v_{in}$ and an out-node $v_{out}$ joined by an edge of integer capacity $b_v$; each link also carries an integer capacity. The super-source $\sigma_F$ feeds one per-node source $\sigma_v$ for each $v\in F$ through an edge of capacity $d_v$, and $\sigma_v$ sends only to the live neighbors $N(v)\setminus F$, so each failed node's demand is capped at $d_v$ and confined to its own neighborhood; each spare is a sink with capacity equal to the number of roles it can still accept. The maximum units of demand served at once then equals the integral max-flow from $\sigma_F$ to the sink set $S$ in that node-split network, and by the max-flow min-cut theorem equals the capacitated $\sigma_F$-$S$ min-cut [63]; this is the tight value of the certificate $C(F,S)$. When all capacities equal one, the result reduces to the vertex-disjoint form of Menger's theorem. Two further properties hold: the value does not decrease when spares are added, since adding a sink cannot lower the max-flow; and by weak duality any feasible flow of value $f$ does not exceed the max-flow, so $f$ is a valid lower bound on $C(F,S)$, and the flow the field builds gives a conservative lower bound computed inside the local field rounds. $\blacksquare$

Theorem 2 (fallback branch: per-scenario guarantee, not optimal). With fixed nominal conductance $c^0$, $c_{min}>0$ on the live graph $G_F$, and Dirichlet boundary $u=1$ on the super-source $\sigma_F$, $u=0$ on $S$, the interior potentials solve the partitioned Laplace system $L_{II}\,u_I=-L_{IB}\,u_B$ on $G_F$, where $u_B$ holds the fixed boundary values; it has a unique solution on each connected component. Let $f_0(F,S)$ be the units of demand that the current-tracing and capacity-reservation procedure serves from the nominal solution for the exact scenario $(F,S)$. The procedure is greedy, so $f_0$ can be smaller than $C(F,S)$: sequential reservation can strand later sources even though a larger flow exists (self-stranding, Section IV-E), and closing the gap to optimum needs an augmenting-path mechanism. Because the gate at steps 8 and 9 takes $f_{out}=\max(f_\theta, f_0)$ and the nominal branch does not depend on the learned weights, the final result satisfies $f_{out}(F,S) \ge f_0(F,S)$ for every scenario, regardless of the learned policy. This is a per-scenario guarantee rather than a bound on an average probability. $\blacksquare$

Lemma 1 (field convergence, independent of the value of the learned weights). Consider the live graph $G_F$ restricted to edges with positive conductance; assume $c_{vw} \in [c_{min},c_{max}]$ with $c_{min}>0$, and that each connected component reaches a Dirichlet boundary node, either a spare in $S$ or, through a node of $B_F$ and its per-node source $\sigma_v$, the super-source $\sigma_F$. Step 5 is a Jacobi iteration for the weighted Laplace system with Dirichlet boundary, and the iteration matrix on the interior nodes is $M=D_c^{-1}A_c$, with $D_c$ the weighted-degree diagonal and $A_c$ the weighted adjacency. $M$ is nonnegative and sub-stochastic: rows adjacent to the boundary sum to less than one, purely interior rows sum to one. Because every interior communicating class reaches a deficient row, a boundary-adjacent node, the spectral radius $\rho<1$, handled per connected block if the subgraph is separated across the boundary. The iteration converges geometrically in the infinity norm at the interior nodes, and the number of rounds for an error of at most $\varepsilon$ is $O(\log(1/\varepsilon)/(1-\rho))$. With conductances uniformly bounded in $[c_{min},c_{max}]$, there is a uniform worst-case lower bound on $1-\rho$ expressed through the conductance ratio $c_{max}/c_{min}$ and the Dirichlet boundary geometry, independent of the specific weight configuration produced by $g_\theta$: on a grid of diameter $D$ the worst-case $1-\rho=\Theta(1/D^2)$, giving $O(D^2\log(1/\varepsilon))$ rounds, while empirically the round count is near-linear in $D$ (Section IV-A). The result is consistent with near-optimal distributed Laplacian solvers [16]. $\blacksquare$

Lemma 2 (asynchronous convergence). The Dirichlet Jacobi matrix $M$ of Lemma 1 is in general not a contraction in the uniform infinity norm, since purely interior rows sum to one. However, because $M$ is nonnegative, sub-stochastic, and each component reaches the boundary, there is a positive weight vector $w$ for which $M$ is a contraction in the weighted sup-norm associated with $w$, with contraction factor $\rho<1$. We use the partially asynchronous model of Bertsekas and Tsitsiklis with three schedule assumptions: each node is updated infinitely often; the gap between two consecutive updates of the same node is bounded above by $B$; and each update reads neighbor values at most $\Delta$ steps old. For a contraction in a weighted sup-norm, the corresponding convergence theorem guarantees that the iteration converges to the same fixed point, and the rate depends on $B$, $\Delta$, and the fairness of the schedule rather than on $\Delta$ alone; under these assumptions the time to reach error $\varepsilon$ is $O((B+\Delta)\log(1/\varepsilon)/(1-\rho))$. $\blacksquare$

